---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { about } from '@/content/copy';
import proof from '@/content/proof.json';
import { site } from '@/content/site';

const stat = (k: string) => proof.stats.find((s) => s.key === k)!;
---

<Base
  title={about.meta.title}
  description={about.meta.description}
  path="/about"
  ogSlug="about"
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>{about.h1}</h1>

    <p class="dek">
      PGManage was not designed in a product meeting, and there is no founding story about
      spotting a market. There was a building, and the rent had to come in.
    </p>

    <p>
      The building is {site.origin.descriptor}. Seven floors, {stat('rooms').display} rooms,
      {stat('beds').display} beds, and on any given day around {stat('residents').display} people
      living in it. Someone has to collect from all of them, every month, and that someone spent
      the first stretch of it doing what everyone in this business does: a notebook, an Excel
      sheet, and a phone with a hundred WhatsApp threads open.
    </p>

    <h2>What we tried first</h2>
    <p>
      Excel went first, because Excel always goes first. It is fine until the month you have a
      partial payment, a late fee, a deposit adjustment and a resident who left on the 9th, and
      then it is four tabs that disagree with each other. We tried a generic property tool next —
      one built for landlords with a handful of flats. It did not know what a bed was. It had no
      concept of a sharing, or a notice period, or a staff member walking around with four days of
      cash in his pocket. So the real work kept happening in WhatsApp and the notebook, and the
      software just held a copy that was already out of date.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thing that broke us was not any single feature. It was the reconciliation. At the end of
      a month you could not say, with a straight face, how much cash had actually come in versus
      how much had been recorded, because the person recording it was the person holding it, and
      the tool had no opinion about that gap. That gap is where the money goes. Everyone in this
      business knows it, and almost nobody has closed it.
    </p>

    <h2>So we wrote the thing we needed</h2>
    <p>
      Not a platform. A tool to collect the rent in two taps and put a name against the cash. Then,
      because the month kept asking for them, the rest arrived one at a time: the ageing buckets
      when the overdue list got long, the vacancy view the month a whole room sat empty because
      nobody logged a notice, the ROI screen the day we argued about whether the three-sharings were
      really carrying the place. Every feature in PGManage is there because a real month made us need
      it. That is the whole design philosophy, and it is the reason the screens look like the job
      and not like a dashboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      We still run the building on it. The numbers on this site —
      {stat('collection').display} of rent collected, {stat('dso').display} to collect,
      {stat('waDelivery').display} of WhatsApp reminders delivered — are that building’s numbers,
      not a demo’s. We do not name it, because it is not a customer and this is not a testimonial.
      It is just where the software comes from, and why it knows what it knows.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we are selling it</h2>
    <p>
      Because every PG owner we have met is fighting the same four fires with the same notebook,
      and it is a genuinely solvable problem that nobody bothered to solve properly for them.
      Software for this business was either built for big student-housing chains and priced for
      them, or it was a landlord app that never heard of a bed. We are neither. We are the operator
      who got tired of the leak and built the plug, and we would like to sell you the plug.
    </p>

    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/demo">Book a demo on your building</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/product">See what it does</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
    padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s9);
  }
  .prose h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5.5vw, var(--t-4xl));
    margin-top: var(--s2);
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .dek {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: var(--s5);
    max-width: 40ch;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    margin-top: var(--s7);
    margin-bottom: var(--s3);
  }
  .prose p {
    color: var(--mut);
    line-height: 1.68;
    margin-top: var(--s4);
    font-size: var(--t-md);
  }
  .prose .dek + p {
    margin-top: var(--s5);
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--s3);
    margin-top: var(--s8);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
</style>
